WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.980
Cargo moves through the Golgi, yet its enzymes stay at their level.

2
00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:08.900
Chapter 1 showed the same problem.

3
00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:12.160
An ER chaperone drifted forward.

4
00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:16.820
A receptor used its KDEL tag to bring it back.

5
00:00:22.367 --> 00:00:25.147
Each flattened sac of the stack is a cisterna.

6
00:00:26.267 --> 00:00:29.147
The cis face receives cargo arriving from the ER.

7
00:00:30.387 --> 00:00:33.627
The trans face passes it on to the trans-Golgi network.

8
00:00:34.947 --> 00:00:40.087
Early sugar enzymes are enriched toward cis, later ones toward trans, and they

9
00:00:40.087 --> 00:00:41.087
overlap.

10
00:00:41.667 --> 00:00:44.507
Picture one is the stable-compartment model.

11
00:00:45.807 --> 00:00:49.867
Each cisterna is long-lived and keeps a characteristic set of enzymes.

12
00:00:51.547 --> 00:00:55.447
Small vesicles bud from one sac and carry cargo forward to the next.

13
00:00:56.727 --> 00:01:01.527
The compartments keep their cis, medial, or trans identities as cargo passes

14
00:01:01.527 --> 00:01:02.527
through.

15
00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:07.060
Rothman's lab rebuilt Golgi transport without intact cells.

16
00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:12.600
VSV G began in donor Golgi from one cell population.

17
00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:18.340
Acceptor Golgi from another population added a sugar after transfer.

18
00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:21.560
That sugar was the assay's readout.

19
00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:25.120
Transport needed ATP and cytosol.

20
00:01:26.267 --> 00:01:28.047
Now weigh what the assay showed.

21
00:01:29.307 --> 00:01:33.967
Does it prove vesicles carry cargo forward, sac by sac, inside one stack?

22
00:01:35.347 --> 00:01:39.607
Separate what the experiment measured from the mechanism its inventors proposed.

23
00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:52.920
Not on its own. The assay inferred transfer between separate Golgi populations from

24
00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:53.920
the sugar readout.

25
00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:57.480
It did not watch cargo move within one stack.

26
00:01:58.660 --> 00:02:02.240
Nor did it identify the carrier that crossed between populations.

27
00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:07.460
And transfer between populations fits the second picture too.

28
00:02:08.483 --> 00:02:11.663
Picture two is the cisternal maturation model.

29
00:02:12.883 --> 00:02:15.083
Here the cisterna itself carries the cargo.

30
00:02:16.403 --> 00:02:18.603
It forms at the cis face with cargo inside.

31
00:02:20.023 --> 00:02:24.743
Then it moves through the stack and ends as trans Golgi and trans-Golgi network.

32
00:02:25.963 --> 00:02:29.883
The compartment changes identity while its cargo remains inside.

33
00:02:31.017 --> 00:02:33.377
So how do the enzymes stay at their level?

34
00:02:34.457 --> 00:02:39.897
They keep being carried backward, in COPI vesicles, to the younger cisterna behind.

35
00:02:41.357 --> 00:02:46.857
Backward recycling replaces early enzymes with later ones, as each cisterna matures.

36
00:02:47.697 --> 00:02:51.317
The sacs flow forward, while the enzymes keep going back.

37
00:02:52.350 --> 00:02:57.010
That is the KDEL retrieval logic: recycling against the flow.

38
00:02:58.250 --> 00:03:00.110
The comparison is only the logic.

39
00:03:01.590 --> 00:03:07.050
Golgi enzymes use different retention and retrieval machinery, not the KDEL

40
00:03:07.050 --> 00:03:08.050
receptor.

41
00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:10.560
Put the two pictures side by side.

42
00:03:11.820 --> 00:03:15.340
In one, the sacs stay put and the cargo hops between them.

43
00:03:16.540 --> 00:03:21.280
In the other, the cargo stays inside a sac that changes character from cis to trans.

44
00:03:22.450 --> 00:03:28.490
Which is right? In the text, predict how fast one filmed cisterna should change.

45
00:03:29.750 --> 00:03:32.950
For stacked mammalian Golgi, the question remains open.

46
00:03:34.390 --> 00:03:38.910
Glick and Luini judged that no single model explains every observation.

47
00:03:40.110 --> 00:03:43.910
They proposed maturation as the best candidate for a shared core.

48
00:03:44.983 --> 00:03:48.683
So how does cargo cross the stack while its enzymes stay put?

49
00:03:50.223 --> 00:03:54.303
Fixed sacs and moving vesicles, or moving sacs and recycled enzymes.

50
00:03:55.463 --> 00:03:57.423
Either way, the cargo goes forward.
